layout "layout.faqir"

block content
  p
    | The
    strong Settings
    | page holds everything that applies to your whole Buildyboy: notifications, limits and warnings, GitHub, telemetry,
    | secret masking, your password and your logged-in devices. Each section has its own
    strong Save
    | button, which shows
    code Saving…
    | and then
    code Saved
    | .
  include "partials/shot.faqir" (assets=site.assets file="settings-desktop.png" alt="The Settings page: Notifications and Budgets, retention and watchers on the left; Telemetry and statusline and Redaction rules on the right." caption="Settings on a computer. On a phone the sections are stacked.")

  h2#notifications Notifications
  p
    | Web Push per device, the test button, the ntfy fallback, quiet hours and the switch for each kind of notification.
    | All of it is explained in
    a(href="/docs/notifications") Notifications
    | .

  h2#budgets Budgets, retention and watchers
  dl
    dt
      strong Hold at Stop (minutes)
    dd
      | With walk mode on, how long a finished turn waits for your next instruction. Default 5. Values above 9 are treated
      | as 9; 0 means no wait. A session's own
      code /bb-walk Nm
      | wins over this.
    dt
      strong Keep raw events (days)
    dd
      | Raw hook events and job output older than this are deleted (the clean-up runs several times a day). Sessions, your
      | prompts, Claude's messages and key events stay. Default 90.
    dt
      strong Session budget (USD)
    dd Warns you when one session has cost about this much. Empty means no limit.
    dt
      strong Job budget (USD)
    dd
      | Warns you when one job approaches this cost. Empty means no limit. The warning fires at 90% of the amount. A job's
      | own Max budget is still what actually stops it.
    dt
      strong Context threshold (%)
    dd The context watcher warns when a session's context is fuller than this. Default 85, from 1 to 100.
  h3#watchers Smart watchers
  p
    | Watchers look at every session once a minute and raise a warning once per problem: a row in the session's timeline
    | and, if switched on in Notifications, a notification.
  dl
    dt
      strong Stale
    dd No event for 10 minutes while working.
    dt
      strong Loop suspicion
    dd The same failing call 3 or more times, or 15 turns with no file changes.
    dt
      strong Budget
    dd A session or job passes its cost threshold. This switch also covers the rate-limit warning (above 80% of Claude's usage window) and the harness "Limit reached" warning.
    dt
      strong Context
    dd The context is above the threshold, or the session has compacted 3 or more times.
    dt
      strong Harness alignment
    dd
      | A harness run's
      code PROGRESS.md
      | "Next" section has not changed for 60 minutes: it may be stuck.

  h2#github GitHub
  p
    | With a GitHub token, Buildyboy follows the pull requests for your sessions' branches and their check results (it
    | looks every 2 minutes), shows them in the session's Git panel and on project pages, and notifies you when checks
    | pass or fail.
  ol
    li
      | On GitHub, create a
      strong fine-grained personal access token
      | with read-only access to
      strong Pull requests
      | ,
      strong Checks
      | (or Commit statuses) and
      strong Contents
      | for the repositories you care about.
    li
      | Paste it into
      strong Personal access token
      | and press
      strong Save
      | .
  p
    | The token is never shown again ("A token is saved. Type a new one to replace it."). Press
    strong Remove token
    | to stop. Only sessions active in the last 7 days, on repositories hosted on github.com, are followed, and only pull
    | requests opened from a branch of the same repository.

  h2#telemetry Telemetry and statusline
  p
    | Two optional extras, each a snippet you add to
    code ~/.claude/settings.json
    | on a machine. Buildyboy never edits that file for you. If the file already has an
    code env
    | section, merge the lines into it.
  h3#otel OpenTelemetry: exact cost, tokens and latency
  p
    | Claude Code can report its own usage figures. Without them Buildyboy estimates cost from what the hooks see; with
    | them the numbers are exact. Copy the snippet from Settings; it looks like this:
  pre
    code
      | {
      |   "env": {
      |     "CLAUDE_CODE_ENABLE_TELEMETRY": "1",
      |     "OTEL_METRICS_EXPORTER": "otlp",
      |     "OTEL_LOGS_EXPORTER": "otlp",
      |     "OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_PROTOCOL": "http/json",
      |     "OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT": "{{ site.url }}",
      |     "OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_HEADERS": "Authorization=Bearer <machine token>"
      |   }
      | }
  p
    | Replace
    code <machine token>
    | with the
    code token
    | value from that machine's
    code ~/.buildyboy/config.json
    | .
  h3#statusline The statusline bridge: context and rate limits
  p
    | Claude Code can show a status line under the prompt. The bridge is a tiny script that passes that information
    | (context fill, cost, model, how much of your 5-hour and 7-day usage windows is used) to Buildyboy, then prints a
    | status line as usual. It powers the context meters and the Rate limits chart in Insights.
  pre
    code
      | {
      |   "statusLine": {
      |     "type": "command",
      |     "command": "node ~/.buildyboy/marketplace/plugin/statusline/bb-statusline.mjs"
      |   }
      | }
  +callout(variant="warning")
    @content
      p
        | The snippet shown in Settings points at
        code ~/.buildyboy/plugin/statusline/…
        | , which does not exist after a normal install. Use the path above, inside
        code marketplace
        | , as written here.
  p
    | Already have a status line of your own? Keep it: put its command in
    code BUILDYBOY_STATUSLINE_NEXT
    | in the same
    code env
    | section, and the bridge runs it and shows its output. Without one, the bridge prints model, context and cost, for
    | example
    code sonnet · ctx 42% · $0.31
    | .

  h2#redaction Redaction rules
  p
    | Before anything is stored, Buildyboy masks secrets with
    code [REDACTED]
    | . Built-in rules already cover API keys (OpenAI-style, Stripe, Google, npm, Slack), GitHub and AWS tokens, JWTs,
    code Bearer
    | headers,
    code password=
    | style values, command-line secrets such as
    code --token
    | , private keys, and the whole content of
    code .env
    | files (except
    code .env.example
    | ,
    code .sample
    | and
    code .template
    | ).
  p Add your own rules for anything else, such as internal tokens:
  ol
    li
      | Press
      strong Add a rule
      | and fill in a
      strong name
      | , a
      strong pattern
      | (a JavaScript regular expression, for example
      code acme-[a-z0-9]{16}
      | ) and optional
      strong flags
      | .
    li
      | Check the line under the rule says
      code Valid
      | , and try it in
      strong Try it on sample text
      | .
    li
      | Press
      strong Save
      | .
  +callout(variant="muted")
    @content
      p
        | Leave
        strong flags
        | empty (or include
        code g
        | ) so every match is masked, not just the first. New rules apply to data that arrives after you save; what is
        | already stored is not changed. The live preview only masks the first match when flags are empty, even though the
        | server masks them all.

  h2#password Password
  p
    | Type your
    strong Current password
    | , then the
    strong New password
    | (at least 8 characters) twice, and press
    strong Change password
    | . Every other logged-in device is logged out; this one stays logged in. Forgot the password? Reset it on the
    | server; see
    a(href="/docs/commands#set-password") bb set-password
    | .

  h2#devices Devices
  p
    | Lists every browser that is logged in, such as "Chrome on Windows", with its address and when it was last active.
    | The current one is marked
    code this device
    | . Press
    strong Log out
    | next to any device you do not recognise, or
    strong Log out of this device
    | to log out here. A login lasts 30 days.
